        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">New Culture Movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> and Tsinghua University, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, with its flourishing publishing sector; some of its writers had first gathered in Tokyo</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Tiger published in Tokyo 1914–1915; New Youth founded 1915; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>'s literary reform essay January 1917; May Fourth protest 1919; the movement split over politics from 1919; overwhelmed by the anti-imperialist and populist violence of the mid-1920s</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Chen Duxiu, founder of New Youth; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a>, chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>, leader of the vernacular faction; Li Dazhao, librarian and editor; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>, author of Diary of a Madman; Gu Jiegang, historian of the Doubting Antiquity School; Chen Hengzhe, Bing Xin and Zhou Zuoren</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">the written vernacular spread and a new literature appeared, while the movement divided: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> and the liberals returned to scholarship, and Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao took part in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a></span></div>
