    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The movement was launched by the writers of New Youth, who blamed Confucian culture for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s weakness and called for “Mr. Science” and “Mr. Democracy” in its place.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>'s argument that “a dead language cannot produce a living literature” led to the written vernacular, baihuawen, replacing Literary Chinese in literature.</li><li>The protest of 4 May 1919 turned a cultural movement into a political one, and its leaders then split: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> urged a return to study, while Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao turned to organised politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first vernacular Chinese fiction was Chen Hengzhe's short story One Day, published in 1917, a year before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>'s Diary of a Madman, which has often been incorrectly credited as the first.</p></aside>
    </section>

