    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">Zhang Shizhao founds the political magazine The Tiger in Tokyo; it runs until October 1915</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">Chen Duxiu founds the Youth Magazine, later retitled New Youth, marking the beginning of the movement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> publishes Preliminary Discussion on Literary Reform in New Youth, urging writers not to imitate the ancients</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a> publishes Diary of a Madman, which implies that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s traditional culture was mentally cannibalistic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> protest the transfer of German rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhou-bay">Jiaozhou Bay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the movement becomes political</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The death of the student Li Chao becomes a rallying cry for anti-feudal and feminist agitation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text">New Culture reformers welcome Margaret Sanger's visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> translates her speech on birth control</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> lectures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and warns that it could encounter trouble by integrating too much progressive and foreign thought</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai.jpg" alt="Yuan Shikai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanShikaiPresidente1915.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo by Rio V. De Sieux [1], Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the 1911 Revolution, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> ended the second revolution of the summer of 1913 and forced many intellectuals into exile, some to Tokyo and others to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. In Tokyo in May 1914 Zhang Shizhao founded the political magazine The Tiger, which ran only until October 1915 but was one of the most influential political journals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> of its time. Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao contributed to it, and its writers grappled with how underlying cultural values and beliefs shape politics, a question that became important in the movement.</p><p>Yuan Shikai, who inherited part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> military after its collapse in 1911, tried to establish order and unity but failed to protect China against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and failed in an attempt to have himself declared emperor. In 1915 the Twenty-One Demands were issued, and six months later it became evident that Yuan intended to restore the imperial system. When he died in June 1916 the collapse of the traditional order seemed complete, and the search for a replacement went deeper than the changes of the previous generations, which had brought new institutions and new political forms.</p><p>The movement arose out of disillusionment with traditional Chinese culture following the failure of the Republic of China to address China's problems.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="Confucius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius,_fresco_from_a_Western_Han_tomb_of_Dongping_County,_Shandong_province,_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous Chinese painter of the Western Han period, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement criticised many aspects of traditional Chinese society and favoured new formulations of Chinese culture informed by modern ideals of mass political participation. Its participants promoted a new society of unconstrained individuals rather than the traditional Confucian system. Chen Duxiu called for “Mr. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>” to be replaced by “Mr. Science” and “Mr. Democracy”, the two symbols of the movement.</p><p>Among its major positions were vernacular writing instead of classical literature, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>'s saying “Speak in the language of the time in which you live”; the emancipation of women; and the view that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is a nation among nations rather than a uniquely Confucian culture. It also promoted democratic and egalitarian values, a national orientation to the future rather than the past, and the re-examination of Confucian texts and ancient classics with modern textual and critical methods, known as the Doubting Antiquity School.</p><p>In the movement's view, Confucian morality repressed the universal and natural experience of sexuality, which they believed was best considered in scientific terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a> contended that divine authorities and superstitions should be overthrown, while seeing in religious aesthetics a potential for achieving transcendence through the appreciation of beauty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hu-shih.jpg" alt="Hu Shih" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hu_Shih_1960_color_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1915 Chen Duxiu founded the Youth Magazine, later retitled New Youth. It began as a small operation but soon became much more influential than The Tiger had been. In 1917 Chen Duxiu and Zhang Shizhao moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> University, where they met the others who made up the movement's community. In January 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> published Preliminary Discussion on Literary Reform in New Youth, with the guideline “Do not imitate the ancients”, and on 18 April 1918 he followed it with Constructive Literary Revolution – A Literature of National Speech.</p><p>On 4 May 1919 students in Beijing aligned with the movement protested at the transfer of German rights over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhou-bay">Jiaozhou Bay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Imperial Japan</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>, which turned a cultural movement into a political one. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a> resigned as principal of Beijing University on 9 May 1919 there was an uproar in the media across the country. The demonstrations first united the leaders, but they then fell out over the role of politics.</p><p>On 16 August 1919 the student Li Chao died of tuberculosis in poverty, after being cut off from her family's wealth for refusing a forced marriage and insisting on higher education. The publication of her private letters made her a martyr among radical students, and a memorial service on 30 November 1919 was held at the Women's Higher Normal School before thousands of guests.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei.jpg" alt="Cai Yuanpei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai_Yuanpei-loc-nodate-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Library of Congress, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The founders clustered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, where they joined the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a>. Chen Duxiu was Dean of the School of Arts and Letters and founder of New Youth, Li Dazhao was librarian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> was a leading figure in the literary revolution; the philosopher Liang Shuming and the historian Gu Jiegang were among the others. Many were classically educated. Li Dazhao campaigned for a new culture, opposed the enshrinement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s constitution and wrote articles promoting Western civilisation, constitutional rule and democracy.</p><p>Hu Shih was considered the leader of the vernacular faction and used the textual study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dream-of-the-red-chamber">Dream of the Red Chamber</a> and other vernacular fiction as a basis for a national language. Scholars such as Zhao Yuanren began studying the varieties of Chinese with the tools of foreign linguistics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>'s essays and short fiction created a sensation: Diary of a Madman implied that China's traditional culture was mentally cannibalistic, and The True Story of Ah Q showed typical Chinese people as weak and self-deceiving. Mao Dun, Lao She and Bing Xin also published their first works in these years, and the movement promoted women writers.</p><p>Foreign thinkers mattered too. John Dewey arrived in 1919 and lectured for a year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> lectured widely to warm crowds. Cai Yuanpei, Li Shizeng and Wu Zhihui developed a Chinese variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The written vernacular was one outcome. In theory it allowed people with little education to read texts, articles and books, though the new vernacular included many foreign words and Japanese neologisms, which made it difficult for many to read. Journalism was modernised in the 1920s: editors and journalists became professionals, and the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> papers such as Shenbao moved away from the advocacy journalism of the 1911 period. Education was high on the agenda. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a> headed a New Education Society, and university students joined the Mass Education Movement of James Yen and Tao Xingzhi, which promoted literacy.</p><p>After May 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>, Cai Yuanpei and other liberals urged demonstrating students to return to the classroom. Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, frustrated with the inadequacy of cultural change, urged more radical political action and used their posts to organise Marxist study groups and the first meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. Li called for “fundamental solutions”; Hu answered with a call for “more study of questions, less study of isms”. Their younger followers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>. Gu Jiegang, who had studied the New History at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, applied it to classical Chinese texts in the Doubting Antiquity Movement.</p><p>New Culture intellectuals had advocated and debated a wide range of cosmopolitan solutions, including science, technology, individualism, music and democracy, and left to the future the question of what organisation or political power could carry them out. The anti-imperialist and populist violence of the mid-1920s soon overwhelmed their intellectual inquiry and culture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians have judged the movement differently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> historians viewed it as a revolutionary break with feudal thought and the seedbed of the leaders who created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> wrote that the May 4th Movement “marked a new stage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s bourgeois-democratic revolution against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and feudalism”. Historians in the west also saw a break between tradition and modernity, but Chinese and foreign historians now commonly argue that its changes had roots going back several generations and were an acceleration of earlier trends. Research also suggests that, besides radical Marxists, there were influential anarchists, conservatives, Christians and liberals.</p><p>Other historians argue that Mao's communist revolution betrayed the movement's spirit of independent expression and cosmopolitanism rather than fulfilling it. Yu Yingshi defended Confucian thought against the New Culture condemnation, reasoning that late imperial China had not been stagnant, irrational and isolated, and that late Qing thinkers were already drawing on the creative potential of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>. Xu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> intellectual who reflects liberal voices, agreed that the movement was the root of the Communist Revolution but valued the outcome differently. He held that the cosmopolitan movement of the 1920s gave way to a “new age of nationalism”.</p><p>Many leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, including Liao Zhongkai, Hu Hanmin and Dai Jitao, took part in the movement, and played a major role in restructuring the party along Soviet lines in 1922–1924.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The New Culture Movement was a Chinese intellectual movement of the 1910s and 1920s, launched by the writers of New Youth, that attacked Confucian tradition and promoted the vernacular, science and democracy. After the May Fourth protest of 1919 its leaders divided over politics.</em></p>

